Q: How to divide a Python script in a shorter script and a module? A: <p>Here is a way to explicitly connect parValue between the two files and in this way address &quot;furas&quot; concern to make the general module connect to the specific parValue defined in the script.</p>
<p>TEST3.py</p>
<pre><code>from function import par, set_parValue

parValue = {}
parValue['V_start'] = 1.0
parValue['VX_start'] = 1.0
parValue['VS_start'] = 10.0

set_parValue(parValue)
</code></pre>
<p>Module function.py</p>
<pre><code>_parValue = None

def set_parValue(parValue):
   &quot;&quot;&quot;Set the dictionary used by the fucntions in this module.&quot;&quot;&quot;
   global _parValue
   _parValue = parValue

def par(*x, **x_kwarg):
   &quot;&quot;&quot; Set parameter values if available in the predefined dictionary parValue. &quot;&quot;&quot;   

   if _parValue is None:
      raise RuntimeError(&quot;No parValue dictionary has been set.&quot;) 

   x_kwarg.update(*x)
   x_temp = {}

   for key in x_kwarg.keys():
      if key in _parValue.keys():
         x_temp.update({key: x_kwarg[key]})
      else:
         print('Error:', key, '- seems not an accessible parameter - check the     spelling')
   _parValue.update(x_temp)
</code></pre>
<p>With this now improved script TEST3.py we get in Jupyter notebook what we expect</p>
